JD9365TX MIPI-DSI panel driver (HT05S-30MI-A1, 5.08" 720x720, 4-lane)
for Raspberry Pi 5 / CM5 running Raspberry Pi OS (Bookworm or later).

Files
  panel-jadard-jd9365tx.c                 kernel module (DRM panel driver)
  vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1-overlay.dts   device tree overlay for the panel

Build outputs
  panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko                kernel module
  vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1.dtbo          compiled overlay

The driver needs kernel 6.12 or newer (it uses `mipi_dsi_multi_context`).

There are two ways to build:
  A. natively on the Pi (simple, slower)
  B. cross-compile from WSL / Ubuntu on x86 (needs only the Pi's `uname -r`)

Either way, finish with "Install on the Pi" below.


## A. Build on the Pi

### A1. Bootstrap (run once)

```
# Bring the system (and kernel) up to date first, so the headers match
sudo apt update
sudo apt full-upgrade -y
sudo reboot
```

After the reboot:

```
# Compiler, make, cpp, etc.
sudo apt install -y build-essential

# Kernel headers for the *running* kernel (provides /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build)
sudo apt install -y linux-headers-$(uname -r)

# Device tree compiler, for building the overlay
sudo apt install -y device-tree-compiler

# Optional: rebuild the module automatically on kernel upgrades
sudo apt install -y dkms
```

Check that the headers are in place:

```
ls /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build/Makefile
```

If `linux-headers-$(uname -r)` is not found, install the meta package that
matches your kernel flavour instead (`uname -r` shows which one):

```
sudo apt install -y linux-headers-rpi-2712   # Pi 5 / CM5 kernel (...-rpi-2712)
sudo apt install -y linux-headers-rpi-v8     # Pi 3/4/5 generic arm64 kernel (...-rpi-v8)
```

### A2. Build

```
make          # module + overlay
make module   # module only
make overlay  # overlay only
make clean
```

## B. Cross-compile from WSL (Ubuntu on x86)

Everything is downloaded from the Raspberry Pi OS package archive; the only
thing needed from the Pi is the output of `uname -r` (and, for some kernel
versions, which OS release it runs; see B2).

The Pi's headers package can't be used directly on x86 (its build helpers are
arm64 binaries), so the module is built against the exact source of the Pi's
kernel, prepared with the Pi's `.config` and `Module.symvers`. No full kernel
build is needed, only `modules_prepare` (a few minutes).

Everything is downloaded and built in `/rpi-build/` on the Linux filesystem
(one subdirectory per kernel version), even if this driver directory is on
the Windows drive. Don't put it under `/mnt/c`: extracting the kernel source
(about 90,000 small files) takes about a minute on the Linux filesystem but a
very long time on `/mnt/c`, with no output while it runs. Windows folders are
also case-insensitive by default, which silently overwrites kernel files
whose names differ only in case (e.g. `xt_DSCP.h` / `xt_dscp.h`).

### B1. Install tools (once)

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y bc bison flex libssl-dev libelf-dev libncurses-dev make \
    crossbuild-essential-arm64 device-tree-compiler dpkg-dev kmod curl xz-utils

# Build directory on the Linux filesystem, owned by you
sudo mkdir -p /rpi-build
sudo chown "$USER:" /rpi-build
```

### B2. Find the Pi's kernel package

Run all of B2 to B5 in the same shell, starting from this driver directory.

```
DRIVER_DIR=$PWD
PI_REL=6.18.50+rpt-rpi-2712        # <-- paste the Pi's `uname -r` here
PI_VER=${PI_REL%%+*}               # e.g. 6.18.50
POOL=http://archive.raspberrypi.com/debian/pool/main/l/linux
DEB_VER=${PI_VER}-1+rpt1           # Debian version of the package (usual case)

# Check the package exists; this also lists any other builds of the same kernel
curl -s $POOL/ | grep -o "linux-headers-${PI_REL}_[^\"]*_arm64.deb" | sort -u
```

**Note: check the curl output against `DEB_VER`.** The usual result is a
line ending in `_${PI_VER}-1+rpt1_arm64.deb` (Raspberry Pi OS 13 "Trixie"),
which matches the default. If it shows something different:

- **A different version** (e.g. `..._6.12.87-1+rpt1~bookworm_arm64.deb`, or
  `-1+rpt1~bpo12+1`, `-1+rpt2`, ...): copy the part between `${PI_REL}_` and
  `_arm64.deb` into `DEB_VER`, e.g. `DEB_VER=6.12.87-1+rpt1~bookworm`.
- **Two lines** (`-1+rpt1` and `-1+rpt1~bookworm`): keep the default if the
  Pi runs Trixie; use `DEB_VER=${PI_VER}-1+rpt1~bookworm` if it runs
  Bookworm.
- **Nothing:** `PI_REL` is mistyped, or the kernel is not a Raspberry Pi OS
  package (e.g. a self-built kernel); this method won't work for it.

The Trixie and Bookworm builds of the same kernel use different compilers and
have different symbol CRCs, so a module built against the wrong one will refuse
to load. If you have the Pi's `uname -a` (or `uname -v`), it names the exact
Debian version, e.g. `... Debian 1:6.12.75-1+rpt1~bookworm (2025-...)`.

### B3. Download the headers and the exact kernel source

```
BUILD=/rpi-build/$PI_REL
mkdir -p "$BUILD" && cd "$BUILD"

# .config, Module.symvers and kernel.release of the Pi's kernel
curl -fLO "$POOL/linux-headers-${PI_REL}_${DEB_VER}_arm64.deb"
dpkg-deb -x "linux-headers-${PI_REL}_${DEB_VER}_arm64.deb" headers
HDR=$PWD/headers/usr/src/linux-headers-$PI_REL
cat $HDR/include/config/kernel.release      # must print $PI_REL

# Kernel source: upstream tarball + Debian/Raspberry Pi patches (~150 MB)
curl -fLO "$POOL/linux_${PI_VER}.orig.tar.xz"
curl -fLO "$POOL/linux_${DEB_VER}.debian.tar.xz"
curl -fLO "$POOL/linux_${DEB_VER}.dsc"
dpkg-source -x "linux_${DEB_VER}.dsc" linux   # applies the patches, incl. rpi/rpi.patch
```

`dpkg-source` may warn that it cannot verify the signature; that is fine.

### B4. Prepare the source tree

```
cd "$BUILD/linux"
export ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu-

cp $HDR/.config .config
# The Pi's .config carries a stale CONFIG_LOCALVERSION ("-v8-16k"); Debian sets
# the real suffix (e.g. "+rpt-rpi-2712") at package build time. Put the real
# suffix in the config so every make in this tree computes the Pi's release.
./scripts/config --set-str LOCALVERSION "${PI_REL#$PI_VER}" --disable LOCALVERSION_AUTO
make olddefconfig
make KERNELRELEASE=$PI_REL modules_prepare
cp $HDR/Module.symvers .

cat include/config/kernel.release           # must print exactly $PI_REL
grep UTS_RELEASE include/generated/utsrelease.h   # must show "$PI_REL"
make -s LOCALVERSION= kernelrelease         # must also print $PI_REL
```

The release string must be the Pi's full `uname -r` (e.g.
`6.18.50+rpt-rpi-2712`), not just the version plus flavour. If any of the
three checks prints something else (e.g. `6.18.50-v8-16k`), the module will
be rejected by the Pi: redo B4 from `cp $HDR/.config .config`.

`olddefconfig` may adjust a few compiler-dependent options (Ubuntu's
cross-gcc is a different version from the one the Pi kernel was built with).
That doesn't affect loading the module: the version string and symbol CRCs
come from the Pi's files.

### B5. Build the driver and copy it to the Pi

```
cd "$DRIVER_DIR"
make KDIR="$BUILD/linux" ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu-

modinfo panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko | grep vermagic   # must start with $PI_REL
scp panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1.dtbo pi@<pi-host>:~
```

After the Pi's kernel is updated, repeat B2 to B4 with the new `uname -r`
(each kernel gets its own directory under `/rpi-build/`). Old ones can be
deleted with `rm -rf /rpi-build/<old uname -r>`.


## Install on the Pi

Run from the directory holding the `.ko` and `.dtbo` (the repo for A, `~` for B).

```
# Kernel module
sudo install -D -m 644 panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko \
    /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/updates/panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko
sudo depmod -a

# Overlay
sudo cp vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1.dtbo /boot/firmware/overlays/
```

Add to `/boot/firmware/config.txt`, then reboot:

```
dtoverlay=vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1
```

Verify after reboot:

```
lsmod | grep jd9365tx
dmesg | grep -i -e dsi -e jd9365
```


